00:00:26.952 --> 00:00:28.410
AMIT GANDHI: This
is the container.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.640
What happens in this, is
biogas gets produced in here.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.190
It goes out the tube
through this set of tubing,

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.310
and into this
container to hold it.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.330
This is just clamped off.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:44.400
And when you unclamp
it, you should

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.490
be able to get biogas
from this and light it.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.500
This has only been going for
a week and a half, or so.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.730
So it may be producing biogas.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.650
If you play with this, you
feel some suction on there.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:01.590
But I don't know how much
gas this one produced yet.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.440
I've been keeping it upstairs
because upstairs is way

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.867
warmer than downstairs
in D lab, so, yeah,

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.450
when we're done with
ours, we're going

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.440
to put them upstairs, as well.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.480
The base looks
something like this.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.700
So the first thing we have to
do is measure out 12 inches

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.680
by 7 inches from here.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.520
And each of your
groups of three is

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.870
going to have to cut
one of these out.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.210
So let's start by measuring it.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.720
I'm going to run up and
grab a completed one.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.595
[INAUDIBLE] so you guys can see.

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:42.970
Should probably
go the other way.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:43.553
So he gets it.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.850
So you want to measure
it 2 inches from here

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.137
and 3 to 1/4 inch or

00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:11.970
AUDIENCE: 3 and 1/2.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.810
AMIT GANDHI: 3 and
1/2 inches from--

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.830
AUDIENCE: OK and then
you think [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:37.813
where's the [INAUDIBLE]
Yeah [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:02:37.813 --> 00:02:39.730
REBECCA SMITH: Basically,
your only ingredient

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.050
in the biogas digester,
is this slurry.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.380
So for your container, you'll--

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.740
for us, we're going
to fill this 3/4 full.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:57.070
And the ratio of water to human,
animal, or vegetable waste

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:02.050
is three parts water to
one part [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.020
And so that is one
thing that might make--

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.390
basically, that's a factor
you'll want to consider.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.320
We were thinking of putting
in a biogas digester where

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.610
I was in Tanzania,
but their water supply

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.680
dries up a few
months a year, which

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.530
meant that just to get
water for their family,

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.560
they would have to
either pay more or walk

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:27.830
a really far distance.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.525
So something like this,
would be a lower priority.

00:03:30.525 --> 00:03:31.900
And there's a good
chance that it

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.820
would dry up and stop working.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.730
And after that, it's really
hard to get it going again.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.910
So the three things
that are really

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:44.680
important for a biogas system
are a good supply of manure,

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.930
having a hot enough
temperature, and having

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.655
a good supply of water.

00:03:48.655 --> 00:03:50.030
So if you have
those three things

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.940
it's a really good solution.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.100
But if one is missing,
then it will be tricky.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.894
So someone want to dig in.

00:03:58.894 --> 00:04:04.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.460
Ooh, it's ripe.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.500
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.308
Less terrifying than
you should expect.

00:04:10.308 --> 00:04:11.850
REBECCA SMITH: Oh,
it's nice and dry.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.320
AUDIENCE: OK, so kind.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:18.465
Are we doing more than one

00:04:18.465 --> 00:04:22.213
REBECCA SMITH: Yeah,
we're doing [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:22.213 --> 00:04:23.380
AUDIENCE: Cold or hot water?

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.150
Lukewarm, oh.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.970
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.220
AUDIENCE: Is that good enough?

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.257
A little more, less?

00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:39.340
REBECCA SMITH: No, we only
want two quarters full.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.965
So you could do a
little tiny bit more.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.960
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
stuck to my jeans.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:05:03.010
[INAUDIBLE]

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.758
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:05:12.983 --> 00:05:16.606
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:16.606 --> 00:05:23.170
Then we need to hot
glue the bottle to

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:23.670
Oh, really?

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.420
Another, we need another
piece like that, right?

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:26.960
That goes.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.340
REBECCA SMITH: [INAUDIBLE]
surprisingly well.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:28.700
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:29.650
REBECCA SMITH: I'm
pretty impressed with us.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:30.605
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:05:30.605 --> 00:05:34.610
So I think this is where the
other tubing, the clear tubing,

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:35.780
is supposed to go.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.670
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:37.730
AUDIENCE: Like
through here, right.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.590
AMIT GANDHI: Need to make
sure this is sealed first,

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:40.430
for whatever.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:42.680
REBECCA SMITH: OK, so we
need another piece of tubing.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.858
And hot glue down [INAUDIBLE]
to the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:47.858 --> 00:05:50.400
AUDIENCE: I feel like the white
[? tape ?] should be smaller.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.880
It should be floating.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.190
I feel like the tube should
be less tall because it's

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.000
kind of in the way right now.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:58.500
Yeah.

00:06:08.275 --> 00:06:10.650
REBECCA SMITH: So one thing
that you can do with the heat

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.960
sealer, is make a soda stack.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.130
So this is used for solar
disinfection of water.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:21.690
So it's one way that
you can purify water.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.730
What you do, is
you can fill any,

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:27.180
basically, clear plastic
container with water.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.540
And then you leave it out in the
sun for about six hours or so.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.220
And it will kill the
bacteria that's in it.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.360
So you might still need to
filter it if it's turbid.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.140
But as one step, that's
actually pretty effective.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:47.862
So actually a D-lab alum,
MIT alum, called Sean Frayne,

00:06:47.862 --> 00:06:49.320
his company is
called [? Haddock ?]

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.870
and they produce these.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.800
So an alternative would be to
just fill maybe a plastic water

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.560
bottle, or this is one.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.685
But it uses more
material than this so

00:07:02.685 --> 00:07:03.820
would be more expensive.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.140
And it's not super easy to
fill if you're pouring water

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.900
from something.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.760
So what he came up
with, is just this bag.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:19.570
Basically, you can
fill the water here.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:23.720
So it'll just go in

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.460
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.070
REBECCA SMITH: Yeah, so
there's a smaller bag here

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.350
that has a hole in it.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.930
We actually, in
the last session,

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.690
spent a while figuring
out exactly how

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.030
the different parts work because
it's a pretty clever design.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:43.910
And then, so basically,
you fold it down

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:47.870
and then the water
is sealed in here.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:51.950
And then after
your six hours, you

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:58.780
can just pour it
out pretty easily.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.430
So it's a pretty clever design.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.250
And this is something that
you can make in the field

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.790
to like every time, I think
Amy brings a heat sealer

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.290
on most of her trips.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.510
Because there are just
a lot of useful things

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:14.350
that you can make.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:25.460
I worked on a pouring dispenser
for my D-lab design project.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:29.830
And we did something
kind of like this,

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:32.140
where it's like this, but
it's just a one-way valve.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:37.210
So the idea isn't that you'll be
able to get it out afterwards.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:40.450
But this was a really
low-cost way for us

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.360
to store the chlorine,
and then keep people

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.040
from getting it out.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.040
Because we didn't want anyone
to be able to siphon it out

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:51.560
or tamper or anything like that.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:56.240
Another thing you can do.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.330
So this is a drip
irrigation kit.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.680
And the way this works, is you
would fill it up with water,

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.320
and then you'd have
a tube coming out.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.260
And from that tube,
are many little straws.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:15.220
And you can direct a
small amount of water

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:16.490
straight to your crops.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.670
So it's a much more
effective way of irrigating.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.370
And it uses a lot less water.

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.550
And someone who I've
worked with in Tanzania,

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.850
named Bernard, he
has actually made

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:32.240
his own heat-sealing
machines, like this.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.590
And so he can make
the tubes that

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:39.250
would come out of this,
just from a heat sealer

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.010
and locally available
plastic rather than having

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.460
to buy that or import it.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:51.340
Another thing you can do to
seal something like this,

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:57.350
if you have male and
female pieces like this,

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:00.910
you can take some rubber
and make a washer.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:05.560
And what you do is, have
a rubber washer layer

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.130
then a plastic or metal washer.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:12.130
And then you could
have your plastic bag.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:16.210
Basically, this
piece is a way to--

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:18.220
and then on the
other side, you'd

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.520
have another rubber
washer, plastic washer.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:26.770
And then you could have a
sealed connection there.

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:29.320
Just as an example of
something else you could do.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:35.170
A way to connect a bag like
this to something else.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:40.270
So the things we have
that you can try out.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:44.720
So this machine and this
one work the same way.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:50.350
It's cool to the
touch, but basically,

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.585
when you push it down,
you'll see a light go on,

00:10:53.585 --> 00:10:54.460
and it makes a click.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:01.250
And then it just
melts the plastic.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:04.350
And you can vary
the heat level here.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:09.350
So in some cases, like for
the [INAUDIBLE] bag like this,

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:11.750
you might have some
seals where you

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.930
want it to go through
two bags, but not three,

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.320
or something like that.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.730
So you can adjust the
level so that it will only

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:24.050
melt through two and
leave the other untouched.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:33.790
And yeah,

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.577
AUDIENCE: Are you
saying that like

00:11:35.577 --> 00:11:36.410
REBECCA SMITH: Yeah.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:39.820
AUDIENCE: So are you
saying that this part?

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:45.087
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:45.087 --> 00:11:49.880
So you can tell the kind
of, so this edge of the bag

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:51.290
is this thing.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:52.530
Yeah

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:53.630
AMIT GANDHI: Yeah.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:56.210
AUDIENCE: And then with this
edge it goes to the little.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:57.200
To the small, yeah.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.100
And so when I do this then,
essentially what happens

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.280
is, that I prevent water
from coming in from here.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:08.720
Because when water hits here,
this part is stuck there.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:12.620
So the other side of this tube,
is one side of this one bag?

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.120
The other side of this?

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:17.290
Yeah, this goes into the bag.

